## std/integrum.pengu — the integer: methods that only make sense on whole
## numbers, over the `Integrum` concept.
##
## `integrum` dota de métodos a **todos** los primitivos enteros de PenguScript
## (`int`, `i8`–`i64`, `u8`–`u64`, `usize`, `isize`, `byte`) mediante un único
## bloque `enchanting shard T where T: Integrum:`. El compilador monomorfiza
## cada método por tipo concreto en tiempo de compilación (`int_is_even`,
## `i64_gcd`, `u32_bit_count`, …), así que no hay vtables, boxing ni coste en
## runtime.
##
## ## Alcance
## `integrum` cubre **solo** lo que tiene sentido en enteros: paridad,
## primalidad, bits, MCD/MCM, divisiones euclidianas y representaciones
## (`to_hex`, `to_bin`, `to_oct`). Los métodos universales (`abs`, `signum`,
## `clamp`, `pow_int`, `min_with`, `max_with`, `twice`, `half`, `round`) viven
## en `std.numerus`; como `Integrum` implica `Num` (y concede igualdad y orden,
## LANGUAGE.md §11.3), esos métodos siguen disponibles dentro de un cuerpo
## acotado por `Integrum`. Los métodos exclusivos de flotantes viven en
## `std.fractio`.
##
## ## Delegación
## `integrum` importa `std.numerus` y delega en él siempre que necesita el
## valor absoluto (`gcd`, `lcm`, `to_hex`, `to_bin`, `to_oct` usan
## `calling self.abs`) en lugar de duplicar el `if self < donum T: return -self`.
## Este módulo es la **fuente de verdad** de la teoría de números entera: las
## funciones `is_prime`, `gcd`, `lcm` y `is_power_of_two` de `std.arithmancy`
## son ahora wrappers de una línea sobre estos métodos. `arithmancy`
## `next_power_of_two` **no** delega: su contrato es "la menor potencia de dos
## `>= n`", mientras que el de aquí es "estrictamente mayor" (`4` → `8`).
##
## ## Memoria
## Solo `to_hex`, `to_bin` y `to_oct` asignan memoria del heap: devuelven un
## `string` nuevo que el llamador debe liberar con `banish` (o `defer banish`).
## El resto de métodos no asigna memoria.
##
## ## Precondiciones
## - `is_multiple_of`, `lcm`, `div_rem`, `div_floor`, `mod_floor`: `other != 0`
##   (división por cero). No paniquean; el comportamiento es el de C.
## - `is_prime`, `next_prime`, `prev_prime`: el algoritmo es O(√n) por división
##   por tentativa con la optimización 6k±1. Viable hasta ~10^12; para
##   criptografía con `u64` grande, usa una librería de primalidad dedicada.
## - `factorial`: el resultado debe caber en `T`. En perfiles de depuración el
##   desbordamiento **con signo** aborta (`-ftrapv`); en release envuelve según
##   la aritmética de C. Límites seguros: `5!` cabe en `u8`, `7!` en `i16`,
##   `12!` en `i32`, `20!` en `i64`.
## - `next_power_of_two`: si `self` ya es la mayor potencia representable, el
##   desplazamiento envuelve a 0.
## - `to_hex`/`to_bin`/`to_oct`: en tipos con signo el resultado es
##   signo-y-magnitud (`-255.to_hex` es `"-ff"`); para el valor mínimo de `T`
##   (`INT_MIN`) la magnitud no es representable y el resultado es indefinido.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## import std.spark
## import std.integrum
##
## weave main into int:
##     var n as int is 12
##     calling spark.println with "{calling n.is_even}"       # true
##     calling spark.println with "{calling n.gcd with 18}"   # 6
##     var h as string is calling n.to_hex                    # "c"
##     calling spark.println with h
##     banish h
##     return 0
## ```

import std.numerus
import std.ward

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Module Version (LANGUAGE.md §19.0: every hand-written std module exports one)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Integrum module version.
const INTEGRUM_VERSION as string is "2.11.0"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private helpers (outside the shard: they do not need a `self`)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Helper privado: un dígito hexadecimal (`0`–`15`) como `string`.
##
## Devuelve un `string` literal (`"a"`–`"f"`) o, para `0`–`9`, un interpolado
## **owned**. El llamador es responsable de liberarlo: `banish` es un no-op sobre
## el literal y libera el interpolado, así que `banish` sobre el resultado es
## correcto en ambos casos. (El runtime no libera nada por su cuenta: el modelo
## de memoria es manual.) Ver `to_hex`.
weave _integrum_hex_digit with d as int into string:
    if d < 10:
        return "{d}"
    if d == 10:
        return "a"
    if d == 11:
        return "b"
    if d == 12:
        return "c"
    if d == 13:
        return "d"
    if d == 14:
        return "e"
    return "f"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Integer-only methods (one body, one C function per concrete receiver)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

enchanting shard T where T: Integrum:

    # ─── Helpers privados ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # NOTA DE DISEÑO: el plan de la fase pedía este helper como un `weave`
    # genérico libre fuera del bloque, invocado con
    # `calling _integrum_is_prime_impl of T with n`. Ese idiom está roto en el
    # codegen actual: pasar el propio parámetro de tipo como argumento explícito
    # de tipo monomorfiza con `T -> T` (no con el receptor concreto) y emite
    # `void*` en lugar del primitivo. Un método `_`-prefijado en el mismo shard
    # sí se monomorfiza por receptor concreto (`is_odd` → `self.is_even` es el
    # patrón ya soportado). Ver el reporte de la fase.

    ## Helper privado: primalidad por división por tentativa 6k±1.
    ##
    ## No forma parte de la API pública (prefijo `_`); `is_prime`, `next_prime`
    ## y `prev_prime` lo usan para no triplicar el algoritmo.
    weave _is_prime_impl into bool:
        if self < (2 to T):
            return false
        if self == (2 to T):
            return true
        if self == (3 to T):
            return true
        if (self % (2 to T)) == donum T:
            return false
        if (self % (3 to T)) == donum T:
            return false
        var k as T is (5 to T)
        # `k <= self / k` and not `k * k <= self`: for a 32-bit receiver the
        # square overflows once `k` reaches ~46341, which made
        # `2147483647.is_prime` (the Mersenne prime 2^31 - 1) report `false`.
        # Dividing keeps every intermediate in range.
        while k <= self / k:
            if (self % k) == donum T:
                return false
            if (self % (k + (2 to T))) == donum T:
                return false
            set k is k + (6 to T)
        return true

    # ─── Predicados ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es divisible por 2.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 4.is_even       # true
    ## (-7).is_even    # false
    ## ```
    weave is_even into bool:
        return (self % (2 to T)) == donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` no es divisible por 2.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 4.is_odd        # false
    ## (-7).is_odd     # true
    ## ```
    weave is_odd into bool:
        return not calling self.is_even

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es una potencia de 2 (1, 2, 4, 8, …).
    ##
    ## `0.is_power_of_two` es `false`. El truco `(n & (n - 1)) == 0` lo hace
    ## O(1).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 1.is_power_of_two    # true
    ## 8.is_power_of_two    # true
    ## 6.is_power_of_two    # false
    ## ```
    weave is_power_of_two into bool:
        if self <= donum T:
            return false
        return (self & (self - (1 to T))) == donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es múltiplo de `other` (o sea, `self % other == 0`).
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `other != 0`. El comportamiento es indefinido si se viola.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var n as int is 12
    ## calling n.is_multiple_of with 4    # true
    ## calling n.is_multiple_of with 5    # false
    ## var z as int is 0
    ## calling z.is_multiple_of with 7    # true
    ## ```
    weave is_multiple_of with other as T into bool:
        return (self % other) == donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es primo (mayor que 1 y sin divisores propios).
    ##
    ## ## Complejidad
    ## O(√n) por división por tentativa con la optimización 6k±1. Viable en la
    ## práctica hasta ~10^12; para criptografía usa primalidad probabilística.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 2.is_prime      # true
    ## 17.is_prime     # true
    ## 18.is_prime     # false
    ## ```
    weave is_prime into bool:
        return calling self._is_prime_impl

    # ─── Bits ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Cuenta los bits puestos a 1 (popcount) del ancho completo de `T`.
    ##
    ## Para tipos con signo se usa la representación en complemento a dos, así
    ## que el bit de signo cuenta: `(-1).bit_count` es el ancho de `T`. El
    ## recorrido está acotado por el ancho, de modo que un valor negativo no
    ## buclea (un desplazamiento aritmético a la derecha nunca llega a 0).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 0b1011.bit_count    # 3
    ## 255.bit_count       # 8
    ## ```
    weave bit_count into int:
        var n as T is self
        var count as int is 0
        var i as int is 0
        var w as int is (size of T) * 8
        while i < w:
            if (n & (1 to T)) == (1 to T):
                set count is count + 1
            set n is n >> 1
            set i is i + 1
        return count

    ## Devuelve el número de bits necesarios para representar `self` (el índice
    ## del bit más alto con valor 1, más uno).
    ##
    ## `0.bit_length == 0`, `1.bit_length == 1`, `255.bit_length == 8`. En
    ## complemento a dos un valor negativo tiene el bit de signo puesto, así que
    ## su longitud es el ancho de `T`: `(-1).bit_length == 32` en un `int`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 1.bit_length      # 1
    ## 255.bit_length    # 8
    ## 256.bit_length    # 9
    ## ```
    weave bit_length into int:
        var n as T is self
        var i as int is 0
        var last as int is 0
        var w as int is (size of T) * 8
        while i < w:
            if (n & (1 to T)) == (1 to T):
                set last is i + 1
            set n is n >> 1
            set i is i + 1
        return last

    ## Cuenta los bits a cero más significativos (por encima del bit más alto
    ## con valor 1), usando el ancho completo de `T`.
    ##
    ## `0.leading_zeros == (size of T) * 8`; siempre se cumple
    ## `leading_zeros + bit_length == ancho`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## # En un int de 32 bits:
    ## 1.leading_zeros      # 31
    ## 255.leading_zeros    # 24
    ## ```
    weave leading_zeros into int:
        return ((size of T) * 8) - calling self.bit_length

    ## Cuenta los bits a cero menos significativos (por debajo del bit más bajo
    ## con valor 1), usando el ancho completo de `T`.
    ##
    ## `0.trailing_zeros == (size of T) * 8`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 8.trailing_zeros     # 3
    ## 12.trailing_zeros    # 2
    ## ```
    weave trailing_zeros into int:
        var w as int is (size of T) * 8
        if self == donum T:
            return w
        var n as T is self
        var count as int is 0
        while (n & (1 to T)) == donum T:
            set count is count + 1
            set n is n >> 1
        return count

    ## Devuelve la menor potencia de 2 estrictamente **mayor** que `self`.
    ##
    ## Para `self == 0` devuelve `1`; para un `self` que ya es potencia de 2
    ## devuelve el doble. El resultado se desborda a `0` si `self` ya es la
    ## mayor potencia representable.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 0.next_power_of_two     # 1
    ## 5.next_power_of_two     # 8
    ## 8.next_power_of_two     # 16
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave next_power_of_two into T:
        var p as T is (1 to T)
        while p <= self:
            set p is p << 1
        return p

    ## Devuelve la mayor potencia de 2 **menor o igual** que `self`.
    ##
    ## Para `self == 0` devuelve `0`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 5.prev_power_of_two     # 4
    ## 8.prev_power_of_two     # 8
    ## 0.prev_power_of_two     # 0
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave prev_power_of_two into T:
        if self == donum T:
            return donum T
        var p as T is (1 to T)
        while (p << 1) <= self:
            set p is p << 1
        return p

    # ─── Divisiones euclidianas ──────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve una tupla `(cociente, resto)` de la división entera, calculando
    ## la división una sola vez.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `other != 0`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var n as int is 17
    ## let (q, r) is calling n.div_rem with 5
    ## # q == 3, r == 2
    ## ```
    weave div_rem with other as T into (T, T):
        return (self / other), (self % other)

    ## División entera redondeada hacia −∞ (a diferencia de `/`, que trunca
    ## hacia cero).
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `other != 0`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var p as int is 7
    ## calling p.div_floor with 2    # 3
    ## var n as int is -7
    ## calling n.div_floor with 2    # -4 (a diferencia de -7 / 2 == -3)
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave div_floor with other as T into T:
        let q as T is self / other
        let r as T is self % other
        if (r != donum T) and ((r < donum T) != (other < donum T)):
            return q - (1 to T)
        return q

    ## Resto con el signo del divisor (no negativo cuando `other > 0`).
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `other != 0`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var n as int is -7
    ## calling n.mod_floor with 3    # 2  (signo del divisor)
    ## var m as int is 7
    ## calling m.mod_floor with 3    # 1
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave mod_floor with other as T into T:
        let r as T is self % other
        if (r != donum T) and ((r < donum T) != (other < donum T)):
            return r + other
        return r

    # ─── MCD / MCM ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Máximo común divisor por el algoritmo de Euclides, **no negativo**.
    ##
    ## Identidades: el MCD de `0` y `n` es `n`; el de `n` y `0` es `n`; el de
    ## `0` y `0` es `0`. El signo se normaliza con `abs` (delegado en
    ## `std.numerus`), así que el MCD de `-12` y `18` es `6`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as int is 12
    ## calling a.gcd with 18   # 6
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave gcd with other as T into T:
        var a as T is self
        var b as T is other
        while b != donum T:
            let t as T is b
            set b is a % b
            set a is t
        return calling a.get_abs

    ## Mínimo común múltiplo, **no negativo**.
    ##
    ## El MCM de `0` con cualquier valor, y de cualquier valor con `0`, es `0`.
    ## Se calcula como
    ## `(self / gcd(self, other)) * other`, que reduce el riesgo de desbordamiento
    ## frente a `self * other / gcd`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as int is 4
    ## calling a.lcm with 6     # 12
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave lcm with other as T into T:
        if self == donum T or other == donum T:
            return donum T
        let g as T is calling self.get_gcd with other
        let p as T is (self / g) * other
        return calling p.get_abs

    # ─── Números primos ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve el menor primo estrictamente mayor que `self`.
    ##
    ## Para `self < 2` devuelve `2`.
    ##
    ## ## Complejidad
    ## O((hueco hasta el siguiente primo) × √n); el hueco medio es O(log n).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 12.next_prime    # 13
    ## 0.next_prime     # 2
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave next_prime into T:
        var n as T is self + (1 to T)
        if n < (2 to T):
            set n is (2 to T)
        while not calling n._is_prime_impl:
            set n is n + (1 to T)
        return n

    ## Devuelve el mayor primo estrictamente menor que `self`.
    ##
    ## Para `self <= 2` devuelve `0` (no hay primo menor).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 100.prev_prime   # 97
    ## 2.prev_prime     # 0
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave prev_prime into T:
        if self <= (2 to T):
            return donum T
        var n as T is self - (1 to T)
        while n >= (2 to T):
            if calling n._is_prime_impl:
                return n
            set n is n - (1 to T)
        return donum T

    ## Devuelve `self!` (factorial).
    ##
    ## `0! == 1`, `1! == 1`, `5! == 120`.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## El resultado debe caber en `T`. En perfiles de depuración el
    ## desbordamiento con signo aborta (`-ftrapv`); en release envuelve según la
    ## aritmética de C. Límites seguros: `u8` → 5, `i16` → 7, `u16` → 8,
    ## `i32`/`u32` → 12, `i64`/`u64` → 20.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## 5.factorial     # 120
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave factorial into T:
        if self <= (1 to T):
            return (1 to T)
        var acc as T is (1 to T)
        var i as T is (2 to T)
        while i <= self:
            set acc is acc * i
            set i is i + (1 to T)
        return acc

    # ─── Representaciones ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # Las tres asignan heap: devuelven un `string` nuevo que el llamador libera
    # con `banish`. En tipos con signo la representación es signo-y-magnitud
    # (`-255.to_hex` es `"-ff"`); para el mínimo de `T` la magnitud no es
    # representable y el resultado es indefinido.

    ## Convierte `self` a hexadecimal **sin prefijo `0x`**, en minúsculas.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## Asigna un `string` nuevo en el heap. **Libéralo con `banish`**.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## Para tipos con signo, `self` distinto del mínimo de `T` (`INT_MIN`).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var n as int is 255
    ## var h as string is calling n.to_hex
    ## calling spark.println with h          # "ff"
    ## banish h
    ## ```
    weave to_hex into string:
        if self == donum T:
            return "0"
        let negative as bool is self < donum T
        var n as T is calling self.get_abs
        var acc as string is ""
        while n != donum T:
            let d as int is (n % (16 to T)) to int
            let hd as string is calling _integrum_hex_digit with d
            let joined as string is "{hd}{acc}"
            banish hd
            banish acc
            set acc is joined
            set n is n / (16 to T)
        if negative:
            let signed as string is "-{acc}"
            banish acc
            return signed
        return acc

    ## Convierte `self` a binario **sin prefijo `0b`**.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## Asigna un `string` nuevo. **Libéralo con `banish`**.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## Para tipos con signo, `self` distinto del mínimo de `T`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var n as int is 5
    ## var b as string is calling n.to_bin
    ## calling spark.println with b          # "101"
    ## banish b
    ## ```
    weave to_bin into string:
        if self == donum T:
            return "0"
        let negative as bool is self < donum T
        var n as T is calling self.get_abs
        var acc as string is ""
        while n != donum T:
            let bit as int is (n & (1 to T)) to int
            let joined as string is "{bit}{acc}"
            banish acc
            set acc is joined
            set n is n >> 1
        if negative:
            let signed as string is "-{acc}"
            banish acc
            return signed
        return acc

    ## Convierte `self` a octal **sin prefijo `0o`**.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## Asigna un `string` nuevo. **Libéralo con `banish`**.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## Para tipos con signo, `self` distinto del mínimo de `T`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var n as int is 8
    ## var o as string is calling n.to_oct
    ## calling spark.println with o          # "10"
    ## banish o
    ## ```
    weave to_oct into string:
        if self == donum T:
            return "0"
        let negative as bool is self < donum T
        var n as T is calling self.get_abs
        var acc as string is ""
        while n != donum T:
            let d as int is (n % (8 to T)) to int
            let joined as string is "{d}{acc}"
            banish acc
            set acc is joined
            set n is n / (8 to T)
        if negative:
            let signed as string is "-{acc}"
            banish acc
            return signed
        return acc

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Helper de test: compara dos `int` con el nombre del módulo ya resuelto.
weave _t_int with got as int, want as int into void:
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with got, want

## Helper de test: compara dos `bool`.
weave _t_bool with got as bool, want as bool into void:
    calling ward.assert_eq_bool with got, want

## Helper de test: compara dos `string`.
weave _t_str with got as string, want as string into void:
    calling ward.assert_eq_string with got, want

test "is_even covers the four quadrants":
    var p as int is 4
    var q as int is 5
    var r as int is -4
    var s as int is -5
    var z as int is 0
    calling _t_bool with (calling p.is_even), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling q.is_even), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling r.is_even), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling s.is_even), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling z.is_even), true

test "is_even works for narrow and wide receivers":
    var a as i64 is 100
    var b as u8 is 3
    calling _t_bool with (calling a.is_even), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling b.is_even), false

test "is_odd is the complement of is_even":
    var p as int is 7
    var q as int is 8
    var r as int is -7
    calling _t_bool with (calling p.is_odd), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling q.is_odd), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling r.is_odd), true

test "is_power_of_two accepts the powers and rejects the rest":
    var a as int is 1
    var b as int is 2
    var c as int is 4
    var d as int is 8
    var e as int is 3
    var f as int is 6
    calling _t_bool with (calling a.is_power_of_two), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling b.is_power_of_two), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling c.is_power_of_two), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling d.is_power_of_two), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling e.is_power_of_two), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling f.is_power_of_two), false

test "is_power_of_two rejects zero and negatives":
    var z as int is 0
    var n as int is -4
    calling _t_bool with (calling z.is_power_of_two), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling n.is_power_of_two), false

test "is_multiple_of handles exact, inexact and zero dividends":
    var a as int is 12
    var z as int is 0
    var n as int is -12
    calling _t_bool with (calling a.is_multiple_of with 4), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling a.is_multiple_of with 5), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling z.is_multiple_of with 7), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling n.is_multiple_of with 4), true

test "is_prime on the small cases":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 2
    var d as int is 3
    var e as int is 4
    var f as int is 17
    var g as int is 100
    calling _t_bool with (calling a.is_prime), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling b.is_prime), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling c.is_prime), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling d.is_prime), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling e.is_prime), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling f.is_prime), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling g.is_prime), false

test "is_prime around a large prime":
    var p as int is 7919
    var q as int is 7920
    calling _t_bool with (calling p.is_prime), true
    calling _t_bool with (calling q.is_prime), false

test "bit_count counts the set bits":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 0b1011
    var d as int is 255
    calling _t_int with (calling a.bit_count), 0
    calling _t_int with (calling b.bit_count), 1
    calling _t_int with (calling c.bit_count), 3
    calling _t_int with (calling d.bit_count), 8

test "bit_count counts the sign bits of a negative":
    var n as int is -1
    calling _t_int with (calling n.bit_count), 32

test "bit_length reports the width of the value":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 255
    var d as int is 256
    calling _t_int with (calling a.bit_length), 0
    calling _t_int with (calling b.bit_length), 1
    calling _t_int with (calling c.bit_length), 8
    calling _t_int with (calling d.bit_length), 9

test "bit_length uses two's complement for negatives":
    var n as int is -1
    calling _t_int with (calling n.bit_length), 32

test "leading_zeros complements bit_length":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 255
    calling _t_int with (calling a.leading_zeros), 32
    calling _t_int with (calling b.leading_zeros), 31
    calling _t_int with (calling c.leading_zeros), 24

test "leading_zeros and bit_length add up to the width":
    var x as int is 7919
    calling _t_int with ((calling x.leading_zeros) + (calling x.bit_length)), 32

test "trailing_zeros counts the low zero bits":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 8
    var d as int is 12
    calling _t_int with (calling a.trailing_zeros), 32
    calling _t_int with (calling b.trailing_zeros), 0
    calling _t_int with (calling c.trailing_zeros), 3
    calling _t_int with (calling d.trailing_zeros), 2

test "next_power_of_two rounds strictly up":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 4
    var d as int is 5
    var e as int is 1023
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_next_power_of_two), 1
    calling _t_int with (calling b.get_next_power_of_two), 2
    calling _t_int with (calling c.get_next_power_of_two), 8
    calling _t_int with (calling d.get_next_power_of_two), 8
    calling _t_int with (calling e.get_next_power_of_two), 1024

test "prev_power_of_two rounds down inclusively":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 4
    var d as int is 5
    var e as int is 1023
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_prev_power_of_two), 0
    calling _t_int with (calling b.get_prev_power_of_two), 1
    calling _t_int with (calling c.get_prev_power_of_two), 4
    calling _t_int with (calling d.get_prev_power_of_two), 4
    calling _t_int with (calling e.get_prev_power_of_two), 512

test "div_rem returns quotient and remainder":
    var a as int is 17
    let (q, r) is calling a.div_rem with 5
    calling _t_int with q, 3
    calling _t_int with r, 2

test "div_rem holds the Euclidean identity":
    var a as int is -17
    let (q, r) is calling a.div_rem with 5
    calling _t_int with ((q * 5) + r), -17

test "div_floor rounds toward minus infinity":
    var p as int is 7
    var n as int is -7
    var q as int is 2
    var m as int is -2
    calling _t_int with (calling p.get_div_floor with q), 3
    calling _t_int with (calling n.get_div_floor with q), -4
    calling _t_int with (calling p.get_div_floor with m), -4
    calling _t_int with (calling n.get_div_floor with m), 3

test "mod_floor takes the sign of the divisor":
    var p as int is 7
    var n as int is -7
    var q as int is 3
    var m as int is -3
    calling _t_int with (calling p.get_mod_floor with q), 1
    calling _t_int with (calling n.get_mod_floor with q), 2
    calling _t_int with (calling p.get_mod_floor with m), -2
    calling _t_int with (calling n.get_mod_floor with m), -1

test "gcd of the classic pair":
    var a as int is 12
    var b as int is 18
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_gcd with b), 6
    calling _t_int with (calling b.get_gcd with a), 6

test "gcd with zero and coprimes":
    var n as int is 15
    var z as int is 0
    var c as int is 9
    var d as int is 28
    calling _t_int with (calling z.get_gcd with n), 15
    calling _t_int with (calling n.get_gcd with z), 15
    calling _t_int with (calling z.get_gcd with z), 0
    calling _t_int with (calling c.get_gcd with d), 1

test "gcd is non-negative for negative inputs":
    var a as int is -12
    var b as int is 18
    var c as int is 12
    var d as int is -18
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_gcd with b), 6
    calling _t_int with (calling c.get_gcd with d), 6

test "lcm of the classic pair":
    var a as int is 4
    var b as int is 6
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_lcm with b), 12
    calling _t_int with (calling b.get_lcm with a), 12

test "lcm with zero is zero":
    var n as int is 9
    var z as int is 0
    calling _t_int with (calling z.get_lcm with n), 0
    calling _t_int with (calling n.get_lcm with z), 0

test "lcm is non-negative for negative inputs":
    var a as int is -4
    var b as int is 6
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_lcm with b), 12

test "next_prime finds the following prime":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 2
    var d as int is 13
    var e as int is 100
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_next_prime), 2
    calling _t_int with (calling b.get_next_prime), 2
    calling _t_int with (calling c.get_next_prime), 3
    calling _t_int with (calling d.get_next_prime), 17
    calling _t_int with (calling e.get_next_prime), 101

test "prev_prime finds the preceding prime":
    var a as int is 2
    var b as int is 3
    var c as int is 100
    var d as int is 1
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_prev_prime), 0
    calling _t_int with (calling b.get_prev_prime), 2
    calling _t_int with (calling c.get_prev_prime), 97
    calling _t_int with (calling d.get_prev_prime), 0

test "factorial on the small cases":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is 5
    var d as int is 10
    calling _t_int with (calling a.get_factorial), 1
    calling _t_int with (calling b.get_factorial), 1
    calling _t_int with (calling c.get_factorial), 120
    calling _t_int with (calling d.get_factorial), 3628800

test "factorial works on a wide receiver":
    var x as i64 is 12
    calling _t_int with (calling x.get_factorial to int), 479001600

test "to_hex on the boundaries":
    var a as int is 255
    var b as int is 15
    var c as int is 16
    var d as int is 0
    var e as int is 4095
    var ha as string is calling a.to_hex
    var hb as string is calling b.to_hex
    var hc as string is calling c.to_hex
    var hd as string is calling d.to_hex
    var he as string is calling e.to_hex
    calling _t_str with ha, "ff"
    calling _t_str with hb, "f"
    calling _t_str with hc, "10"
    calling _t_str with hd, "0"
    calling _t_str with he, "fff"
    banish ha
    banish hb
    banish hc
    banish hd
    banish he

test "to_hex is sign and magnitude for negatives":
    var x as int is -255
    var h as string is calling x.to_hex
    calling _t_str with h, "-ff"
    banish h

test "to_bin on the boundaries":
    var a as int is 5
    var b as int is 0
    var c as int is 255
    var ba as string is calling a.to_bin
    var bb as string is calling b.to_bin
    var bc as string is calling c.to_bin
    calling _t_str with ba, "101"
    calling _t_str with bb, "0"
    calling _t_str with bc, "11111111"
    banish ba
    banish bb
    banish bc

test "to_oct on the boundaries":
    var a as int is 8
    var b as int is 7
    var c as int is 63
    var oa as string is calling a.to_oct
    var ob as string is calling b.to_oct
    var oc as string is calling c.to_oct
    calling _t_str with oa, "10"
    calling _t_str with ob, "7"
    calling _t_str with oc, "77"
    banish oa
    banish ob
    banish oc

test "integrum composes with numerus on the same receiver":
    var x as int is -12
    var a as int is calling x.get_abs
    var c as int is calling x.get_clamp with 0, 100
    var p as int is calling x.get_pow_int with 2
    calling _t_int with a, 12
    calling _t_int with c, 0
    calling _t_int with p, 144

test "is_prime survives the square root of a 32-bit maximum":
    # Regression: `k * k <= self` overflowed near the end of the trial
    # division for 2^31 - 1, so the one Mersenne prime in `int` range was
    # reported composite.
    var big as int is 2147483647
    calling _t_bool with (calling big.is_prime), true

test "is_prime still rejects its neighbours":
    var even as int is 2147483646
    var other as int is 2147483645
    calling _t_bool with (calling even.is_prime), false
    calling _t_bool with (calling other.is_prime), false

# --- In-place mutation vs the pure `get_` form -------------------------------

test "next_power_of_two mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 5
    calling x.next_power_of_two
    calling _t_int with x, 8
test "get_next_power_of_two returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 5
    var y as int is calling x.get_next_power_of_two
    calling _t_int with y, 8
    calling _t_int with x, 5
test "prev_power_of_two mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 5
    calling x.prev_power_of_two
    calling _t_int with x, 4
test "get_prev_power_of_two returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 5
    var y as int is calling x.get_prev_power_of_two
    calling _t_int with y, 4
    calling _t_int with x, 5
test "div_floor mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 17
    calling x.div_floor with 5
    calling _t_int with x, 3
test "get_div_floor returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 17
    var y as int is calling x.get_div_floor with 5
    calling _t_int with y, 3
    calling _t_int with x, 17
test "mod_floor mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 17
    calling x.mod_floor with 5
    calling _t_int with x, 2
test "get_mod_floor returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 17
    var y as int is calling x.get_mod_floor with 5
    calling _t_int with y, 2
    calling _t_int with x, 17
test "gcd mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 12
    calling x.gcd with 18
    calling _t_int with x, 6
test "get_gcd returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 12
    var y as int is calling x.get_gcd with 18
    calling _t_int with y, 6
    calling _t_int with x, 12
test "lcm mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 4
    calling x.lcm with 6
    calling _t_int with x, 12
test "get_lcm returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 4
    var y as int is calling x.get_lcm with 6
    calling _t_int with y, 12
    calling _t_int with x, 4
test "next_prime mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 8
    calling x.next_prime
    calling _t_int with x, 11
test "get_next_prime returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 8
    var y as int is calling x.get_next_prime
    calling _t_int with y, 11
    calling _t_int with x, 8
test "prev_prime mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 8
    calling x.prev_prime
    calling _t_int with x, 7
test "get_prev_prime returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 8
    var y as int is calling x.get_prev_prime
    calling _t_int with y, 7
    calling _t_int with x, 8
test "factorial mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 5
    calling x.factorial
    calling _t_int with x, 120
test "get_factorial returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 5
    var y as int is calling x.get_factorial
    calling _t_int with y, 120
    calling _t_int with x, 5
test "chained mutations on one receiver":
    var x as int is 20
    calling x.gcd with 12      # 4
    calling x.lcm with 6       # 12
    calling x.twice            # 24
    calling _t_int with x, 24
